V+=[
X(23,'GUARD AT RĀMA’S DOOR',HS('Hanuman standing guard at a palace door','RAKHAVĀRE','crown'),
 'You are the guard at Rāma’s door: no one enters without your permission.',
 [('rāma duāre','<b>at Rāma’s door</b>'),('tuma rakhavāre','<b>you are the keeper</b>'),('hota na ājñā binu','<b>without your order there is no</b>'),('paisāre','<b>entering</b>')],
 'This verse explains a custom: in many temples Hanumān stands at the entrance, and pilgrims to Ayodhyā visit Hanumāngaṛhī before the Rāma temple. Hanumān is the dvārapāla, the gatekeeper.',
 'The idea is gentle, not exclusive. A gatekeeper in Indian courts was the one who introduced visitors to the king; befriending him was the way in. So devotees approach Rāma through Hanumān, as Sugrīva and Vibhīṣaṇa did. Some teachers add an inner reading: the door is the heart, and devotion (Hanumān) lets the Lord in, or lets the mind reach him. Ājñā means command or permission; paisāra is entry.',
 'Respect the people at the door. They often decide more than the people inside.',
 'He keeps <b>Rāma’s door</b>.'),
X(24,'WHY BE AFRAID?',HS('Hanuman sheltering a frightened child','RACCHAKA','heart'),
 'All happiness comes to one who takes refuge in you. With you as protector, why fear anyone?',
 [('saba sukha lahai','<b>gains every happiness</b>'),('tumhārī saranā','<b>in your refuge</b>'),('tuma racchaka','<b>you the protector</b>'),('kāhū ko ḍaranā','<b>why fear anyone?</b>')],
 'Saranā (śaraṇa), refuge, is the same word as in Rāma’s promise to Vibhīṣaṇa: whoever comes once for refuge is protected. Hanumān inherits that promise and extends it to his own devotees.',
 'This is the most quoted verse for children afraid of the dark, and for adults afraid of much more. The second line is a question, not a statement: kāhū ko ḍaranā, “why be afraid of anyone?” It invites the frightened person to answer it. Psychologists might call it reframing; devotees call it faith. Either way the verse gives a short phrase to hold onto when fear rises.',
 'When fear comes, ask the question in the verse. Then answer it.',
 'With him beside you, <b>why fear</b>?'),
X(25,'ONLY YOU CAN HOLD YOUR POWER',HS('Hanuman roaring, the three worlds shaking','HĀṂKA','storm'),
 'You alone can hold back your own brilliance; at your roar the three worlds tremble.',
 [('āpana teja','<b>your own power</b>'),('samhāro āpai','<b>you yourself can contain</b>'),('tīnoṃ loka','<b>the three worlds</b>'),('hāṃka teṃ kāṃpai','<b>tremble at your roar</b>')],
 'Vālmīki describes Hanumān’s roars more than once: on Mount Mahendra before the leap, in the Aśoka grove as he destroys it, and on his return, when the waiting vānaras hear him from far off and know he has succeeded.',
 'Samhāro can mean to withdraw, to control, or to bear. Read one way, no one else can handle Hanumān’s power; only he can. Read another, he alone knows how to restrain it, which recalls the sages’ curse that kept his strength hidden until it was needed. Both readings say the same thing: great strength needs self-control, and in Hanumān the strength and the control live together.',
 'The test of strength is not using it. It is knowing when not to.',
 'Only he can <b>hold</b> his own power.'),
X(26,'NO GHOST COMES NEAR',HS('Hanuman’s name driving shadowy figures away','MAHĀBĪRA','lamp'),
 'Ghosts and evil spirits do not come near when the name Mahāvīra is spoken.',
 [('bhūta pisāca','<b>ghosts and goblins</b>'),('nikaṭa nahiṃ āvai','<b>do not come near</b>'),('mahābīra','<b>“Mahāvīra”</b>'),('jaba nāma sunāvai','<b>when the name is spoken</b>')],
 'Bhūtas are spirits of the dead; piśācas are flesh-eating goblins of folklore. Belief in them was, and is, widespread, and Hanumān is the deity people call on against them.',
 'The verse is a classic of protective chanting. Many say it on dark roads, in empty houses or before sleep. For those who do not believe in ghosts, teachers offer another reading: the bhūtas are our past (bhūta also means “what has been”), our memories and regrets, and the piśācas our cravings, which lose their grip when the mind is filled with courage. Mahāvīra, “great hero”, is the name chosen: the name of courage itself.',
 'Fears grow in silence. Saying something brave out loud helps.',
 'His <b>name</b> keeps the shadows off.'),
X(27,'DISEASE AND PAIN GO',HS('Hanuman with the herb mountain, a sick person rising','NĀSAI ROGA','mountain'),
 'Disease is destroyed and all pain removed for one who keeps repeating the name of the brave Hanumān.',
 [('nāsai roga','<b>disease is destroyed</b>'),('harai saba pīrā','<b>all pain is taken away</b>'),('japata nirantara','<b>by chanting continuously</b>'),('hanumata bīrā','<b>brave Hanumān’s name</b>')],
 'The healing verse follows from the sañjīvanī story: the one who brought the life-herb for Lakṣmaṇa is invoked against illness. Tradition holds that Tulasīdāsa wrote the Hanumān Bāhuk when his own arm was in pain.',
 'Japata nirantara, unbroken repetition, is the method: japa is the steady repeating of a name or mantra, often on a rosary. Many hospitals in India have a small Hanumān shrine, and families recite this verse at the bedside. As a doctor-reader will know, the verse is about comfort and courage as well as cure; it sits beside medicine, not against it. Tulasīdāsa himself sought both prayer and remedy.',
 'Look after the body and the spirit together. Neither does the other’s job.',
 'Chant, and <b>pain</b> lessens.'),
X(28,'FREED FROM TROUBLE',HS('Hanuman lifting a chain off a devotee','MANA · KRAMA · BACANA','chain'),
 'Hanumān frees from trouble anyone who fixes on him in mind, deed and word.',
 [('saṅkaṭa teṃ','<b>from trouble</b>'),('hanumāna chuḍāvai','<b>Hanumān frees</b>'),('mana krama bacana','<b>mind, action and speech</b>'),('dhyāna jo lāvai','<b>whoever fixes attention</b>')],
 'This is the source of Hanumān’s name Saṅkaṭmocan, “remover of trouble”, and of the Saṅkaṭmocan Aṣṭak, a separate hymn of eight verses.',
 'Mana, krama, bacana (manas, karma, vacana), mind, deed and word, are the classical three channels through which a person acts. The condition is that all three point the same way. Saying the prayer while thinking of something else, or praying and then acting against it, divides a person. The verse asks for wholeness: think of him, act for him, speak of him. Trouble, the teachers say, often comes from that very division.',
 'Let your thoughts, words and actions agree. Much trouble ends there.',
 'Mind, deed and word, <b>together</b>.'),
X(29,'RĀMA THE ASCETIC KING',HS('Rama as a king in bark clothes, Hanuman serving','TAPASVĪ RĀJĀ',rama=True),
 'Rāma, the ascetic king, is above all; and you carried out all his work.',
 [('saba para','<b>above all</b>'),('rāma tapasvī rājā','<b>Rāma, the ascetic king</b>'),('tina ke kāja','<b>his tasks</b>'),('sakala tuma sājā','<b>you accomplished all</b>')],
 'Tapasvī rājā is a striking title: a king who lived as an ascetic. Rāma ruled even in exile, in bark clothes, and in Ayodhyā ruled without luxury. Tulasīdāsa’s Rāma is both renunciate and king.',
 'The verse puts Rāma first, saba para, above all, and Hanumān second, as the one who does his work. Even in a hymn to Hanumān, the order of things is kept: the servant is great because the master is greater. Some editions read “saba para rāma rāya siratājā”, Rāma the crown of kings; the sense is the same. Sājā means arranged, set in order, accomplished well.',
 'The best helpers make their leader’s work look easy. That is a skill in itself.',
 'He did <b>all</b> Rāma’s work.'),
X(30,'ANY WISH FULFILLED',HS('A devotee with a lamp, many small wishes rising like sparks','MANORATHA','lamp'),
 'Whoever brings you any other wish obtains the boundless fruit of life.',
 [('aura manoratha','<b>any other desire</b>'),('jo koī lāvai','<b>whoever brings</b>'),('soi amita','<b>he, boundless</b>'),('jīvana phala pāvai','<b>gets life’s fruit</b>')],
 'After the spiritual gifts, the hymn turns to ordinary hopes: a job, a marriage, a child, an exam. Manoratha, “the chariot of the mind”, is a wish or desire.',
 'Indian devotion has never been embarrassed to ask for worldly things. The Gītā itself lists four kinds of devotees, including the one who seeks wealth and the one in distress, and calls all of them noble. Here the promise goes further: not just the wish, but amita jīvana phala, a boundless fruit of life. Teachers read this as a hint that the devotee who comes for small things leaves with something greater.',
 'Ask for what you need. Stay open to getting something better.',
 'Bring any wish; take away <b>more</b>.'),
X(31,'IN ALL FOUR AGES',HS('Hanuman standing across four bands of time','CĀROṂ JUGA','wheel'),
 'Your glory spreads through all four ages; your fame lights up the world.',
 [('cāroṃ juga','<b>all four yugas</b>'),('paratāpa tumhārā','<b>your glory</b>'),('hai parasiddha','<b>is famous</b>'),('jagata ujiyārā','<b>lighting the world</b>')],
 'The four yugas are the ages of the world: Satya, Tretā, Dvāpara and Kali. Hanumān lived in the Tretā with Rāma; met Bhīma in the Dvāpara of the Mahābhārata; and as a cirañjīvī lives on in the Kali age.',
 'Some traditions extend his presence to the first age too, as Śiva’s portion or as Vāyu’s power since creation. The verse prepares for the next ones: if he is present in every age, he is present now, and the devotee of today can call on him as directly as Rāma did. Ujiyārā, light, recalls the first caupāī’s ujāgara: the hymn keeps returning to Hanumān as a light.',
 'Some help never goes out of date. Courage and loyalty are among them.',
 'His glory spans <b>all four ages</b>.'),
X(32,'GUARDIAN OF THE GOOD',HS('Hanuman protecting sages from a demon','SĀDHU SANTA','sage'),
 'You protect sages and saints, destroy the demons, and are Rāma’s darling.',
 [('sādhu santa ke','<b>of sādhus and saints</b>'),('tuma rakhavāre','<b>you are the protector</b>'),('asura nikandana','<b>destroyer of demons</b>'),('rāma dulāre','<b>beloved of Rāma</b>')],
 'This verse echoes the Gītā’s promise of the Lord’s descent: to protect the good and destroy the wicked (4.8). Hanumān carries out that very task for Rāma.',
 'Dulāre is a tender, everyday word, used for a much-loved child. After the grand titles of messenger, hero and gatekeeper, Tulasīdāsa calls Hanumān Rāma’s pet, his darling. The contrast is deliberate: terrible to demons, tender to Rāma. The sādhus and santas he guards include, by tradition, Tulasīdāsa himself, Samartha Rāmadāsa and Rāghavendra Tīrtha, saints who credited Hanumān with their protection.',
 'Be fierce in defending good people, and gentle with those you love.',
 'Guard of the good, <b>Rāma’s darling</b>.'),
X(33,'SĪTĀ’S BOON',HS('Sita blessing Hanuman with raised hand','JĀNAKĪ MĀTĀ','gift'),
 'You give the eight powers and the nine treasures: such is the boon Mother Jānakī gave you.',
 [('aṣṭa siddhi','<b>eight supernatural powers</b>'),('nau nidhi','<b>nine treasures</b>'),('ke dātā','<b>giver of</b>'),('asa bara dīna jānakī mātā','<b>this boon Mother Sītā gave</b>')],
 'In the Rāmacaritamānas, Sītā in the Aśoka grove blesses Hanumān: may he be ageless, immortal, full of virtue and dear to Rāma. Later tradition extends the blessing: he may give the siddhis and nidhis to others.',
 'The eight siddhis of yoga are aṇimā (becoming tiny), mahimā (becoming huge), garimā (heavy), laghimā (light), prāpti (reaching anything), prākāmya (getting what one wishes), īśitva (lordship) and vaśitva (control). Hanumān shows several in the Rāmāyaṇa: tiny for Sītā, huge for the leap, too heavy for Bhīma to lift his tail. The nine nidhis are Kubera’s treasures, such as padma and mahāpadma. Note the giver: not Rāma but Sītā, Jānakī, “daughter of Janaka”, called Mother.',
 'The best gifts come from people we have helped in their darkest hour.',
 '<b>Sītā’s blessing</b>: powers to give.'),
X(34,'THE ELIXIR OF RĀMA',HS('Hanuman holding a pot of nectar marked with Rama’s name','RĀMA RASĀYANA','light'),
 'You hold the elixir of Rāma’s name; may you always remain the servant of the Lord of the Raghus.',
 [('rāma rasāyana','<b>the elixir of Rāma</b>'),('tumhare pāsā','<b>is with you</b>'),('sadā raho','<b>always remain</b>'),('raghupati ke dāsā','<b>servant of the Raghu lord</b>')],
 'Rasāyana is an Āyurvedic term: a rejuvenating tonic that restores youth and long life. Tulasīdāsa uses it for Rāma’s name: the medicine that heals the soul.',
 'The second line can be read two ways. As a blessing: “may you always be Rāma’s servant”, what Hanumān wants most. Or as a description: “you are always Rāma’s servant”. Either way, the verse links the healer of verse 27 with this deeper medicine: the herb saved Lakṣmaṇa’s body, the name saves the self. Many sādhus call japa of Rāma’s name rāma-rasāyana, a lifelong tonic.',
 'Find the thing that renews you, and keep it close.',
 'He holds the <b>elixir</b> of Rāma’s name.'),
X(35,'THROUGH YOU, RĀMA',HS('A devotee singing, Hanuman guiding him towards Rama',None,'heart',rama=True),
 'Through devotion to you one reaches Rāma, and forgets the sorrows of birth after birth.',
 [('tumhare bhajana','<b>by your worship</b>'),('rāma ko pāvai','<b>one attains Rāma</b>'),('janama janama ke','<b>of birth after birth</b>'),('dukha bisarāvai','<b>sorrows are forgotten</b>')],
 'This is the heart of Hanumān’s place in theology: he is not an end but a way. Worshipping him leads to Rāma, just as meeting him led Sugrīva and Vibhīṣaṇa to Rāma.',
 'Bhajana means worship, especially devotional singing. The second line speaks of rebirth: sorrows accumulated over many lives are not paid off one by one but forgotten, dissolved. In Vaiṣṇava teaching this is grace again: the soul is freed not by the arithmetic of karma but by the Lord’s love. The guru-like role of Hanumān returns: the teacher’s worth is the place he leads to.',
 'Good teachers point beyond themselves. Follow where they point.',
 'Through him, <b>to Rāma</b>.'),
X(36,'AT LIFE’S END',HS('A soul rising towards a city of light','RAGHUBARA PURA','light'),
 'At the end of life one goes to Rāma’s city, and wherever one is born after, one is called a devotee of Hari.',
 [('anta kāla','<b>at the time of death</b>'),('raghubara pura jāī','<b>goes to Rāma’s abode</b>'),('jahāṃ janma','<b>where (if) born</b>'),('hari-bhakta kahāī','<b>is known as Hari’s devotee</b>')],
 'The verse speaks of death, and of what follows. Raghubara pura, Rāma’s city, is Sāketa, the heavenly Ayodhyā of Vaiṣṇava theology.',
 'The second line covers both possibilities: if the devotee is not freed at once, they will be born again as a devotee, keeping their love across lives. The Gītā says something similar of the yogī who falls short (6.41–43): he is born in a good family and takes up the path again. For many families this verse is chanted at the bedside of the dying, as a comfort that the journey continues in good hands.',
 'Live so that the way you are remembered is the way you would want to begin again.',
 'At the end, <b>Rāma’s city</b>.'),
X(37,'NO OTHER GOD NEEDED',HS('Hanuman alone in a shrine, a devotee content','SARBA SUKHA','lamp'),
 'One need not hold any other god in mind: Hanumān alone gives every happiness.',
 [('aura devatā','<b>other deities</b>'),('citta na dharaī','<b>need not keep in mind</b>'),('hanumata sei','<b>Hanumān himself</b>'),('sarba sukha karaī','<b>gives all happiness</b>')],
 'This verse is sometimes misread as dismissing other gods. In context it is the language of devotion, which always speaks of its beloved as enough.',
 'Tulasīdāsa honoured Śiva, Gaṇeśa, Durgā and many others in his works; he was not exclusive. The verse means that devotion to Hanumān alone is sufficient: one need not run from temple to temple. Indian devotion calls this ekānta bhakti, one-pointed love, and it does not deny that other doors exist. Some editions read “citta na dharaī” as “need not worry”, giving the same sense.',
 'Depth beats breadth. One faithful practice does more than many scattered ones.',
 'He alone is <b>enough</b>.'),
X(38,'TROUBLES CUT AWAY',HS('Hanuman cutting a rope that binds a devotee','BALABĪRĀ','sword'),
 'Troubles are cut away and all pain vanishes for one who remembers the mighty hero Hanumān.',
 [('saṅkaṭa kaṭai','<b>troubles are cut</b>'),('miṭai saba pīrā','<b>all pain is erased</b>'),('jo sumirai','<b>whoever remembers</b>'),('hanumata balabīrā','<b>Hanumān, strong hero</b>')],
 'The promise of verses 27 and 28 returns, framing the last section of the hymn. Repetition is part of the Cālīsā’s design: it is meant to be chanted, and the refrain-like returns steady the chanter.',
 'Sumirai (smaraṇa) is remembrance, the gentlest form of devotion: not ritual, not even chanting aloud, just keeping him in mind. Smaraṇa is the third of the nine forms of bhakti. The verse ends the list of benefits with the simplest condition of all. Balabīrā joins bala, strength, and bīra (vīra), hero: the strong one who protects.',
 'Remember who you can call on. Half of courage is memory.',
 'Remember him and <b>troubles fall away</b>.'),
X(39,'BE KIND, LIKE A GURU',HS('Hanuman as a teacher, a disciple bowing','GURUDEVA','book'),
 'Victory, victory, victory to you, Lord Hanumān! Be kind to me, as my guru would.',
 [('jai jai jai','<b>victory, victory, victory</b>'),('hanumāna gosāīṃ','<b>Lord Hanumān</b>'),('kṛpā karahu','<b>show grace</b>'),('gurudeva kī nāīṃ','<b>as a divine teacher does</b>')],
 'The triple jai marks the start of the closing section, the phalaśruti, which tells the benefits of reciting the hymn. Gosāīṃ (gosvāmī), “master of the senses”, is a title of honour; Tulasīdāsa himself is often called Gosvāmī Tulasīdāsa.',
 'The hymn began with the guru’s feet; it ends asking Hanumān to be the guru. The request is not for favours but for the kind of grace a teacher gives: correction, patience and guidance, not just gifts. In the guru tradition, the teacher’s kṛpā is the one thing that cannot be earned. Asking Hanumān to be that teacher completes the circle the first dohā opened.',
 'Find someone who will teach you, not just please you. Then listen.',
 'Be my <b>guru</b>, Hanumān.'),
X(40,'A HUNDRED RECITATIONS',HS('A devotee reciting with a rosary, a broken chain at his feet','SATA BĀRA','chain'),
 'Whoever recites this a hundred times is freed from bondage and gains great happiness.',
 [('jo sata bāra','<b>whoever a hundred times</b>'),('pāṭha kara koī','<b>recites</b>'),('chūṭahi bandi','<b>is freed from bonds</b>'),('mahā sukha hoī','<b>great joy comes</b>')],
 'A hundred recitations is a traditional vow. Some do it in a single day, some as 108 for the rosary’s count, and some read “sata” as “sat”, truly, meaning “whoever recites it truly”.',
 'Bandi means bondage: literally prison, and some traditions say a prisoner chanting it was freed. Spiritually it is the bondage of karma and saṃsāra. A popular story tells that Tulasīdāsa, jailed by a ruler, composed or recited the Cālīsā and that an army of vānaras besieged the jail until he was freed; this is legend, but it shows how the verse was understood. Both readings of “sata”, a hundred or truly, point to commitment.',
 'Repeat what matters until it changes you. Habit is a kind of prayer.',
 'Recite it, and <b>bonds</b> loosen.'),
X(41,'ŚIVA IS WITNESS',HS('Shiva with crescent moon nodding over a devotee reading','SĀKHĪ GAURĪSĀ','moon'),
 'Whoever reads this Hanumān Cālīsā will gain success: Śiva, Gaurī’s lord, is witness.',
 [('jo yaha paḍhai','<b>whoever reads this</b>'),('hanumāna cālīsā','<b>Hanumān Cālīsā</b>'),('hoya siddhi','<b>success comes</b>'),('sākhī gaurīsā','<b>Gaurī’s lord (Śiva) is witness</b>')],
 'The only place the hymn names itself. Gaurīśa, “lord of Gaurī (Pārvatī)”, is Śiva, called to witness the promise. In Tulasīdāsa’s Rāmacaritamānas, Śiva is the first narrator of the Rāma story, so he is the natural witness.',
 'Calling Śiva as witness also closes the loop with verse 8, “Śaṅkara suvana”: if Hanumān is Śiva’s own, Śiva vouches for him. A witness (sākṣī) in Indian law made a promise binding. Siddhi here means success or fulfilment in general, not just the yogic powers of verse 33.',
 'Make your promises in front of someone who will hold you to them.',
 '<b>Śiva</b> is witness.'),
X(42,'LIVE IN MY HEART',HS('A poet bowing, Hanuman placing his hand on the poet’s heart','HARI CERĀ','heart'),
 'Tulasīdāsa is forever the servant of Hari: O Lord, make your home in my heart.',
 [('tulasīdāsa','<b>Tulasīdāsa</b>'),('sadā hari cerā','<b>ever Hari’s servant</b>'),('kījai nātha','<b>O lord, make</b>'),('hṛdaya maṃha ḍerā','<b>your dwelling in my heart</b>')],
 'Indian poets sign their work in the last verse, a convention called the bhaṇitā or chāp. Tulasīdāsa signs as Hari’s cerā, a humble word for a servant or disciple. This signature is the main basis for attributing the hymn to him.',
 'Some scholars note that the Cālīsā does not appear in early manuscript lists of Tulasīdāsa’s major works and question whether he wrote it; tradition holds firmly that he did. Either way the request is the same: “make your ḍerā, your camp, your home, in my heart.” A ḍerā is a simple dwelling, a tent: the poet asks for nothing grand, only that Hanumān stay.',
 'Ask for presence, not presents.',
 'Make my heart <b>your home</b>.'),
X(43,'THE CLOSING PRAYER',HS('Hanuman with Rama, Lakshmana and Sita in his heart, blessing',None,'heart',rama=True),
 'Son of the Wind, remover of troubles, embodiment of all that is auspicious: with Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa and Sītā, dwell in my heart, king of the gods.',
 [('pavanatanaya saṅkaṭa harana','<b>son of the Wind, remover of trouble</b>'),('maṅgala mūrati rūpa','<b>form of all auspiciousness</b>'),('rāma lakhana sītā sahita','<b>together with Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa, Sītā</b>'),('hṛdaya basahu sura bhūpa','<b>dwell in my heart, king of gods</b>')],
 'The closing dohā mirrors the opening two. It is usually followed in recitation by shouts of “bolo siyāvara rāmacandra kī jaya, pavanasuta hanumāna kī jaya”, and then an ārati.',
 'The hymn ends where verse 10 placed Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa and Sītā: in a heart. But now it is the devotee’s heart, not Hanumān’s. The request is that Hanumān come in and bring his household with him, the reverse of the torn-chest image. Sura bhūpa, king of the gods, is the highest title in the hymn, given at the very end. Maṅgala mūrati, the form of auspiciousness, is how many temples greet him.',
 'Let the people you admire live in you. Their courage becomes yours.',
 'Live in my heart, <b>with Rāma</b>.'),
]
